## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical IPC path design, per-core locality optimization, and safe buffer migration under memory pressure in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Per-core allocator**: Each CPU core maintains a local free-list of buffers to minimize cache-line bouncing and lock contention
- **Lock-free CAS synchronization**: Compare-and-swap operations enable atomic updates without global locks
- **Cross-core buffer migration**: Moving buffers between cores to balance load while maintaining locality
- **Quotas and fairness**: Ensuring no single client monopolizes buffer resources

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch (C-like) for acquire_buffer and migrate_buffer
typedef struct buf { 
    int id; 
    int state; 
    struct buf *next; 
} buf_t;

typedef struct core_pool { 
    buf_t *free; 
    atomic_int quota; 
} core_pool_t;

buf_t *acquire_buffer(int core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[core];
    buf_t *b;
    do {
        b = atomic_load(&p->free);
        if (!b) return NULL;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &b, b->next));
    atomic_fetch_sub(&p->quota, 1);
    return b;
}

void migrate_buffer(buf_t *b, int to_core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[to_core];
    buf_t *old_head;
    do {
        old_head = atomic_load(&p->free);
        b->next = old_head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &old_head, b));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving I/O across tenants is common in cloud environments; scalable, low-latency throttling under bursty workloads is critical for fair resource allocation and performance isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Token bucket per-tenant: enforces long-term rate while allowing short-term bursts
- I/O scheduler integration (WFQ): throttled requests become blocked, enabling fair scheduling across tenants
- Burst handling: burst tolerance B_i absorbs temporary demand spikes
- Starvation prevention: weighted guarantees, timeout thresholds, and deadline-aware throttling ensure no tenant is indefinitely blocked

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t rate;        // bytes per second
    uint64_t burst;       // max bucket capacity
    uint64_t tokens;      // current token count
    uint64_t last_refill; // timestamp of last refill
    spinlock_t lock;
    wait_queue_t waitq;
} tenant_t;

void refill_tokens(tenant_t *t) {
    uint64_t now = get_time_ns();
    uint64_t elapsed = now - t->last_refill;
    uint64_t added = (elapsed * t->rate) / NS_PER_SEC;
    t->tokens = min(t->burst, t->tokens + added);
    t->last_refill = now;
}

bool admit_io(tenant_t *t, size_t sz) {
    bool granted = false;
    spin_lock(&t->lock);
    refill_tokens(t);
    if (t->tokens >= sz) {
        t->tokens -= sz;
        granted = true;
    }
    spin_unlock(&t->lock);
    return granted;
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Single-tenant steady-state**: Verify throughput matches configured rate b_i
2. **Burst test**: Submit requests exceeding rate, confirm burst tolerance absorbs spikes
3. **Multi-tenant fairness**: Run concurrent tenants with different weights, measure bandwidth distribution
4. **Mixed CPU/I/O workload**: Inject CPU-bound processes alongside I/O tenants to detect starvation
5. **Starvation detection**: Enqueue high-priority tenant behind low-priority bulk tenant, verify latency bounds
